# Settings of the Kassa server on this machine. The server reads them as environment variables
# (node --env-file=...). This file holds the database password: it lives outside the repository
# (C:\kassa\config\kassa.env on Windows), only administrators and the service accounts may read it,
# and it is never committed.
#
# deploy/setup-database.mjs writes it for you, with a password of its own. Edit by hand only to
# change a setting.

NODE_ENV=production
HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=3000
DATABASE_URL=postgres://kassa:CHANGE-ME@127.0.0.1:5432/kassa

# The reverse proxy (Caddy) runs on this machine and tells the server the real address of each
# client in X-Forwarded-For. Without this line every client looks like 127.0.0.1, and a stranger's
# wrong passwords would lock every login out, the owner's too.
TRUST_PROXY=127.0.0.1

# Days a session lives without use (default 90).
# SESSION_DAYS=90

# Copies of the database (deploy/backup.mjs, deploy/windows/backup.ps1). Write the values without quotes.
# Where the copies go, a full path: a second disk is the point, a copy on the disk that fails is no help.
# Without this line backup.ps1 uses C:\kassa\backups.
# BACKUP_DIR=D:\kassa-copies
# How many copies to keep (default 14); older ones are deleted after each new copy.
# BACKUP_KEEP=14
# The folder with pg_dump and pg_restore, if they are not found by themselves.
# PG_BIN=C:\Program Files\PostgreSQL\16\bin

# The token of the Telegram bot of the Mini App (from @BotFather). Unset: signing in inside Telegram is off.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
